{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::execute` receives a context for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node` and computes its\ncompleted answer:\n\n```rust,ignore\nuse std::future::Future;\n\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Select inputs through cx and compute the completed answer.\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        cx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAn `Ok` value is the answer defined by\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait.answer`.\nAn `Err` value reports a [validation error](running-criteria.md#habeas-req-requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ValidationError).",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.execute` is the prescribed `Criterion::execute` signature. The clause is: `fn execute(self, cx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;`. A behavior-preserving alternative would violate it. One example is a `&self` receiver. Another is a boxed `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>` return. A third is a result that is not `Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>`. So the signature is a real structural obligation.\n\nThe requirement's `Implements` edge points to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#trait[Criterion]/fn[execute]`, and it already carries the `{impl}` marker. I read that declaration. It takes `self` by value and a `&mut ValidationContext<'_, D>`. It returns `impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send`. The parameter is named `ctx` instead of `cx`, which is discretionary. This is the same signature the requirement prescribes.\n\nThe supplied review root, `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#trait[Criterion]/fn[version]`, does not relate to `execute`. It is the declaration for `criterion_trait.version`. It supplies no evidence for this requirement, and it needs none. `fn[execute]` already covers the signature.\n\nThe remaining clauses state only observable behavior: the `Ok` value is the completed answer defined by `criterion_trait.answer`, and the `Err` value reports a validation error. The `Ok` type bound belongs to `criterion_trait.answer`, which is cited context. `Domain` implementations and `Criterion` implementors supply the behavior.\n\nI found no missing root or gap.\n\nbehavioral: The obligations in `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.execute` are: (a) `execute` gets a context for the criterion's source node, (b) an `Ok` value is the completed answer, and (c) an `Err` value is a validation error. The marked tests cover each one.\n\n(a) Source-node context: the `Review` test criterion's `execute` calls `run_review`. That reads the node from `ctx.node_id()` and reads that node's contents. `runner_does_not_store_a_missing_agent_error_and_supports_intermediate_calls` runs `verify` for node 1. It asserts that the stored dependency is `Dependency::Node(1, fp(\"root\"))`, so the context was for the source node. The same test shows an intermediate `ask` made inside `execute` works. `concurrent_single_node_validations_keep_separate_traces` also checks that each node's execution gets its own context. It is not marked for `execute`, but the marked tests already establish this obligation, so that is not a gap.\n\n(b) `Ok` answers: the same runner test asserts `value.output().result` after `verify`. `validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync` checks that `verify` returns the criterion's output (17 and 23). It also checks that stored and loaded answers round-trip, and that the futures are `Send`.\n\n(c) `Err` values: `resolution_storage_and_callback_errors_are_not_judgments` shows an error returned from inside `execute` (`Graph(\"missing\")`) is returned as a `ValidationError`, and no record is stored. `ending_without_a_corrected_submission_is_an_agent_error` shows an agent failure inside `execute` is returned as `ValidationError::Agent`. The runner test shows `NoAgentConfigured` is returned and nothing is stored.\n\nThese cover the requirement's observable behaviors.",
  "references": "051c9606718bc803545dbcaf4e6a4a8037d32b6589bab6d791bfffe3b3284738",
